     <h2>10. Proposed MSSQL Tables</h2>
     <p style="font-size:13.5px;">All passthrough today — no DB persistence in Hermes. Existing <code>Customer</code> table stays untouched. <b>Infobip connection settings (<code>BaseUrl</code>, <code>ApiKey</code>) stay in <code>appsettings.json</code></b> — moving them to the DB would require runtime decryption on every send, with no upside given a single per-deployment key. The <code>InfobipConfiguration</code> table below stores only <b>observed/derived state</b> (health-check results, fingerprints, sync metadata), never the secret itself.</p>
+    <p style="font-size:13.5px;"><b>Scope note (revised 2026-05-19).</b> The earlier draft proposed an <code>InfobipProvisioningJob</code> audit/retry log to capture every entity, number, and configuration call Hermes makes against Infobip. That table is <b>dropped</b> — the surface it was meant to cover is now small (one-time bootstrap + idempotent re-reads), each operation already writes <code>LastSyncAt</code> / <code>LastError</code> on its own row, and standard application logging is sufficient for post-mortems. <code>InfobipWhatsappTemplate</code> is added in its place to back the (currently passthrough) WA templates surface — list / status / dedup all become local DB queries.</p>
     <p style="font-size:13.5px;"><b>Naming convention:</b> every Infobip-related table uses the <code>Infobip</code> prefix (PascalCase in SQL/EF, <code>INFOBIP_</code> in the ER diagram below). This keeps the integration layer cleanly grouped and easy to grep.</p>
